  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday: temps will increase ahead of a cold front with highs \npushing into the 80s. Afternoon and evening humidity will drop \nbelow 20 percent for southern areas as winds increase to 10 to 20 \nmph, gusting to 30 mph in the presence of dry fuels. A Red Flag \nWarning in effect from Noon until 8 PM MDT Thursday. \n\nTonight through Friday: A cold front will push through the region\nwith increasing rain showers. Wetting rains are possible(20-50% \nchance) with the steadier convection but will be fairly hit-or- \nmiss and confined to the SW over Petroleum and Phillips counties. \n\nSaturday: Expect a brief pause in rain with temps remaining well \nbelow seasonal norms. \n\nSunday morning through Monday morning: Temps will remain below\nnormal as an overunning event begins with Pacific Moisture. SW\nflow will treack a disturbance across the region and forma Low\npressure center somewhere around Wyoming with the areas north of \nit receiving the bulk of the precip. Currently This includes\nnortheast Montana, but if this track moves south along with the \nlow we could quickly become shut out of all rain being to the\nsouth as well.\n\nTuesday onward coold temps remain in place through mid-week when a\nsignal for a ridge to the west brings a chance for a chinook to \nwarm us back up to seasonal norms sometime around Wednesday to \nFriday.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nA mini deviation to NBM for MinRH was performed along the \nsouthern border to match up with the BYZ office for the Red Flag \nin place this afternoon. \n\nConfidence is HIGH for dry and windy conditions raising fire \nweather concerns on today. \n\nConfidence is LOW for timing and placement of specific showers \nthrough Friday.",
